{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And a bewildering.</b> From the same root as נבוכים (Exodus 14:3):" "<b>That breaks down.</b> An involved form [of the root], or 'destroys.' And note: it [uses the metaphor of] overturning a wall, like 'and uproot it from the land of the living' (Psalms 52:7) — the parallel root applies to wall and to root alike." "<b>And a shouting to the mountain.</b> Some say [it is] like 'they cry out, but there is no deliverer' (II Samuel 22:42); and some say that the shouting reaches the mountain."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra's Hebrew reads מהפך, 'overturning/inverting'; the exact grammatical sense of his argument here depends on a technical usage that is tersely stated, so the rendering is hyper-literal."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:54:39.154502682Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}